   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">What Daily Nighttime Lights Can and Cannot Show About Power Outage and Recovery: Winter Storm Uri in Harris County</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">What Daily Nighttime Lights Can and Cannot Show About Power Outage and Recovery: Winter Storm Uri in Harris County</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Texas</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Texas</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Winter Storm Uri; nighttime lights; Black Marble; power outage; recovery detection; observation availability; placebo test; social vulnerability; spatial regression</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Daily nighttime light (NTL) images are widely used to map power outages and recovery</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Daily nighttime light (NTL) images are widely used to map power outages and recovery</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">but clouds and quality screening leave gaps. We examined what NASA Black Marble data show about the February 2021 Winter Storm Uri outage in Harris County</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">but clouds and quality screening leave gaps. We examined what NASA Black Marble data show about the February 2021 Winter Storm Uri outage in Harris County</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Texas. When only high-quality retrievals were used</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Texas. When only high-quality retrievals were used</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">16 February was the only usable night during the outage</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">16 February was the only usable night during the outage</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and the next were 19 to 21 February. On 16 February</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and the next were 19 to 21 February. On 16 February</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">5112 of 13</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">5112 of 13</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">846 developed pixels (37%) were below half their pre-storm brightness</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">846 developed pixels (37%) were below half their pre-storm brightness</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and 92% of those with an observed return were lit by 21 February. Return dates between these nights are unobservable. Recovery dates from a detector-based workflow depend on smoothing</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and 92% of those with an observed return were lit by 21 February. Return dates between these nights are unobservable. Recovery dates from a detector-based workflow depend on smoothing</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">persistence and aggregation choices; in a controlled experiment with known recovery dates and flag-0 observations only</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">persistence and aggregation choices; in a controlled experiment with known recovery dates and flag-0 observations only</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">an unsmoothed rule requiring three consecutive calendar nights has a median detection delay of 12 to 15 days. Compared with 20 ordinary clear nights</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">an unsmoothed rule requiring three consecutive calendar nights has a median detection delay of 12 to 15 days. Compared with 20 ordinary clear nights</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">16 February dimming was far more extensive and clustered. However</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">16 February dimming was far more extensive and clustered. However</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">the estimated average total impact of the Social Vulnerability Index was 10.6 percentage points of reference radiance (95% interval $-$32.0 to 49.8)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">the estimated average total impact of the Social Vulnerability Index was 10.6 percentage points of reference radiance (95% interval $-$32.0 to 49.8)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">within the range estimated on ten ordinary pre-storm nights ($-$31.9 to 26.8). Single-night community comparisons need an ordinary-night baseline.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">within the range estimated on ten ordinary pre-storm nights ($-$31.9 to 26.8). Single-night community comparisons need an ordinary-night baseline.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
